                "name": "nitrogen & phosphorus loading",
                "level": -20,
                "indicator": "Combined sewer overflow (CSO) annual discharge volume, paired with wastewater nitrogen loading to receiving waters",
                "value": "Hunts Point WRRF (Bronx): biological nitrogen removal retrofit cut nitrogen discharge from ~22,000 lbs/day to ~12,000 lbs/day (~45% reduction), part of the East River WRRF upgrade program.",
                "year": 2025,
                "target": "Reduce CSO volume by 1.67 billion gallons/year by 2040 and spend $3.5B on green infrastructure by 2045 (2023 CSO Consent Order Modification, DEC Case CO2-20110512-25 modifying CO2-20000107-8). Nitrogen: Long Island Sound TMDL (EPA-approved 2001) requires an aggregate 58.5% reduction in human-generated nitrogen vs. a 1990 baseline; the 2010 Jamaica Bay Nitrogen Consent Agreement targeted ~50% reduction in WRRF nitrogen loading to the bay.",
                "context": "The Bronx: Hunts Point WRRF (Bronx): biological nitrogen removal retrofit cut nitrogen discharge from ~22,000 lbs/day to ~12,000 lbs/day (~45% reduction), part of the East River WRRF upgrade program.\n\nCitywide for comparison: ~18 billion gallons/year of combined sewage discharged citywide through 398 CSO outfalls (~60% of NYC is combined-sewered). Nitrogen: the four East River WRRFs (Wards Island, Hunts Point, Bowery Bay, Tallman Island) cut aggregate nitrogen discharge by >58% after a $1B+ upgrade program; the four Jamaica Bay WRRFs (Jamaica, 26th Ward, Coney Island, Rockaway) cut nitrogen loading to Jamaica Bay from a historic ~54,000 lbs/day to a currently reported ~18,000-20,000 lbs/day.\n\nThis is a wastewater-and-CSO story, not an agricultural-runoff story — the sharp structural contrast with Petaluma, where the same dimension is scored on groundwater nitrate near farms and septic systems. NYC's 14 WRRFs and 398 combined-sewer outfalls mean nitrogen and pathogen loading are concentrated at engineered point sources tied to rain events, not diffuse land-based leaching. The policy levers are capital-intensive: WRRF biological nutrient removal retrofits (East River, Jamaica Bay), CSO storage tunnels (Newtown Creek, Flushing Bay), and the green infrastructure program, which the city is currently behind schedule on. Caveat: the citywide 18 BGY figure and the 398-outfall count are the most current published DEP/DEC figures, but a single up-to-date, citywide nitrogen-loading total across all 14 WRRFs (as opposed to per-facility figures of varying vintage) was not found in this search.",
                "source": "City Limits, 'City Expands East River & Long Island Sound Cleanup Effort'",
                "sourceUrl": "https://citylimits.org/city-expands-east-river-long-island-sound-cleanup-effort/",
                "screenshot": null,
                "confidence": "low",
                "geographicScale": "borough",
                "globalLens": {
                    "level": 65,
                    "indicator": "NYC's embodied/consumption-based nitrogen footprint via its imported food supply (no verified city-specific study found; scaled from national per-capita averages as an order-of-magnitude proxy)",
                    "value": "Not directly measured for NYC. The U.S. average per-capita nitrogen footprint is reported as roughly 30-41 kg N/person/year (dominated by food production), per the N-Print project (University of Virginia). Applying that national average to NYC's ~8.3M residents implies a rough order-of-magnitude footprint of roughly 250,000-340,000 metric tons N/year — this is an extrapolation from a national figure, not a measured NYC number, and should be treated as illustrative only.",
                    "context": "NYC's nitrogen and phosphorus footprint is transmitted globally through its food system: importing produce, grain, meat, and dairy from farms that use synthetic fertilizer manufactured from mined phosphate rock (a finite resource concentrated in Morocco/Western Sahara) and fossil-fuel-derived nitrogen. A 2020 peer-reviewed 'community nitrogen footprint' methodology applied to Baltimore City (~19,000 metric tons N/year, ~30 kg N/capita/year, 73% from food production) is the closest published municipal-scale analog and shows this kind of analysis is feasible for a U.S. city; it has not, to my knowledge, been done for NYC. Separately, NYC exports its wastewater biosolids (roughly 500,000+ tons/year across all 14 WRRFs) largely to landfill and historically to out-of-state land application (Colorado, Florida, 1992-2010); each ton landfilled instead of land-applied represents phosphorus that is not being returned to agricultural soils, a lost-recovery issue tied to the finite global phosphate-rock reserve.",
                    "source": "N-Print project (University of Virginia); Wang, Castellanos-Beltran et al. 'A community nitrogen footprint analysis of Baltimore City, Maryland' (IOPscience, 2020) as methodological analog",
                    "sourceUrl": "https://n-print.org/YourNFootprint"
                },
                "comparisonNote": "Petaluma's nitrogen & phosphorus story is groundwater nitrate near agricultural land and septic systems (most wells below the 10 mg/L drinking-water standard, with 4-7 mg/L hotspots near ag boundaries, scored level 20). NYC's is an engineered wastewater-and-CSO story roughly three orders of magnitude larger: 18 billion gallons/year of combined sewage through 398 outfalls citywide and 14 WRRFs whose historic point-source nitrogen loading required a $1B+ East River upgrade program and a separate 2010 Jamaica Bay consent agreement — there is essentially no diffuse agricultural-runoff analog in NYC's version of this dimension, and no groundwater-nitrate analog either, since NYC draws its water from upstate reservoirs rather than local wells.",
                "boroughs": null,
