                    "indicator": "Embodied land-use/deforestation footprint of NYC's food, timber, and commodity consumption (proposed; not yet quantified for NYC)",
                    "value": "Not quantified for NYC. Best available global evidence: beef (cattle/buffalo, ~27% of the total), oil palm, and soy are the top three agricultural commodities driving tropical deforestation worldwide; these three commodities account for roughly 60% of 21st-century tropical deforestation from agricultural expansion.",
                    "context": "NYC's population of 8.3M, its role as a global wholesale hub (Hunts Point produce, meat, and fish markets), and its concentration of corporate food/commodity buyers give it an outsized indirect land footprint relative to its physical size — the inverse framing of the local-ecological lens above, where the city's built density is a land-conservation act. This footprint is transmitted through supply chains (imported beef, soy-fed livestock products, palm oil in packaged food, tropical hardwoods and paper), not through direct land conversion within the five boroughs. It is conceptually the land-use analog to the well-documented gap between NYC's production-based GHG inventory and its higher consumption-based footprint. No source was found that isolates NYC (or even the NY metro) from national U.S. per-capita averages, so any NYC-specific number here would be an estimate presented as fact — instead this is flagged as a research gap. NYC's ~200,000+ acres of protected upstate watershed land (Catskill/Delaware system) is the one large, well-quantified land-protection footprint NYC holds outside its own boundaries, but it protects drinking-water quality rather than offsetting consumption-driven deforestation, so it is reported here for completeness rather than folded into a global-footprint number.",
                    "source": "Forest Trends / Nature Reviews Earth & Environment, 'The global deforestation footprint of agriculture and forestry' (2025); no NYC-specific source located",
